! R23-9694 (7030 CE Introductory Programming, Vol. 2) printed p.51,
! section 6.18.3 TRANSMIT, Example 3, transcribed at IBM's own
! addresses.  The page's words:
!
!   "Example 3: Assume that location 500 contains a particular
!   configuration which, for test purposes, the customer engineer
!   wishes to store in location 501 through 600.  This result can be
!   achieved by the following program steps:
!
!     Name     Instruction            Remarks
!     PREP     LCI, $X7, 100.0        Place count of 100 in XR7.
!     DO IT    T, $X7, 500.0, 501.0   500 to 501, 501 to 502, 502 to
!                                     503, etc.
!     NEXT     Continuation of program"
!
! THE OVERLAP IS THE WHOLE POINT, and it is what makes this a sharper
! test than a plain block move.  Source and destination differ by one
! word, so each word is WRITTEN BEFORE IT IS READ as the next source:
! 500 goes to 501, then 501 -- now holding 500's configuration --
! goes to 502, and so on.  One word is smeared across a hundred.
!
! A model that gathered all hundred source words before storing any,
! or that copied from the high end down, would move the ORIGINAL
! contents of 501-599 up by one instead and leave a quite different
! memory.  The page states which it must be: the facing Example 2
! says of a move that must NOT propagate, "backward transmission must
! be used so that each location is used for sending before it is used
! for receiving" -- so forward TRANSMIT is defined to do exactly the
! propagation this deck checks.  (Example 2's TBI, Transmit Backward
! Immediate, is not implemented; it is a feature request, not a test.)
!
! WHAT THIS IS THE FIRST OF.  The multi-word TRANSMIT with a DIRECT
! count was built on 2026-10-18 from Reference Manual printed p.30
! and MCP printed p.4 line 28, and driven by a deck written here and
! by MCP itself.  This is its first exercise from an independent
! source, and the first anywhere that depends on the copy ORDER
! rather than only on the count and the endpoints.
!
! LCI, $X7, 100.0 puts a count of 100 in X7's count field; "T, $X7"
! then takes that as a full-word count (printed p.30: "the field J
! ... specifies an index register.  The count field, bits 28-45, of
! the index word in this register is used as a full word count").
!
! WHAT IS CHECKED.  Location 500 keeps its configuration, 501 and 600
! -- the two ends of the written range -- hold it too, 550 in the
! middle holds it, and **601 does not**.  That last one is the fence:
! a transmit that ran one word long would be invisible to every other
! check.  601 reads 0xAAAA... because that is what the harness fills
! unwritten core with, so the check is also a statement that nothing
! wrote there at all.

! ONE INJECTION, MEASURED: the transmit made to move one word fewer
! (`remaining: xmitCount - 2`) leaves location 600 holding 0xAAAA...
! and moves NOTHING else -- 500, 501, 550 and the 601 fence all still
! pass.  That is precisely what the far-end check exists for, and it
! is why checking only "some words were copied" would not have been
! worth writing.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

PREP    LCI, $X7, 100.0          ! place count of 100 in XR7
! This assembler takes T's J operand as a BARE INTEGER where the
! listings write "$7" -- see decks/mcp_bootstrap_p5_p6.strap's
! substitution note.  The page's "T, $X7" is therefore "T, 7" here,
! and it is the only departure from the printed statements.
DOIT    T, 7, 500.0, 501.0       ! 500 to 501, 501 to 502, 502 to 503
NEXT    BEW, $                   ! "continuation of program"

! Location 500's "particular configuration", at IBM's own address.
        SLC, 500.0
CONFIG  DD(BU,64,8), (8)0110642547423257157360

        END, PREP
